WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.953
Ownership answers the question of who is responsible for a resource.

2
00:00:03.953 --> 00:00:10.781
That resource might be memory, a file handle, a socket, a lock, a cryptographic context, or a temporary object.

3
00:00:10.781 --> 00:00:14.734
Lifetime answers when that object or resource is valid to use.

4
00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:19.765
Many C++ security and reliability issues are really ownership and lifetime issues in disguise.

5
00:00:19.765 --> 00:00:22.640
RAII helps by tying cleanup to object lifetime.

6
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.874
A file wrapper closes the file in its destructor.

7
00:00:25.874 --> 00:00:29.827
A lock guard releases the lock when the guard leaves scope.

8
00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:33.421
A container releases its allocation when the container is destroyed.

9
00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:40.249
This pattern makes cleanup happen during normal returns, early returns, and exceptions without duplicating cleanup code across every path.

10
00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:45.639
Smart pointers can express ownership more clearly than raw pointers when dynamic ownership is needed.

11
00:00:45.639 --> 00:00:47.436
A unique_ptr communicates single ownership.

12
00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:53.545
A shared_ptr communicates shared ownership, though it should not be used just to avoid thinking about design.

13
00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:57.858
A weak_ptr can break cycles or observe shared ownership without extending lifetime.

14
00:00:57.858 --> 00:01:01.811
Move semantics allow ownership to transfer from one object to another.

15
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:06.483
That is powerful, but reviewers should know what state remains after a move.

16
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:15.467
Moved-from objects are still valid for operations allowed by their type, but the program should not assume they retain their old value unless the

17
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.904
type explicitly promises it.

18
00:01:16.904 --> 00:01:22.295
Raw pointers and references still have a place as non-owning views or interoperability boundaries.

19
00:01:22.295 --> 00:01:27.326
The important rule is that non-owning access must not outlive the owned object.

20
00:01:27.326 --> 00:01:35.950
Passing a reference into a callback, storing a pointer in a long-lived object, or launching asynchronous work with borrowed data all deserve careful

21
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:36.669
lifetime review.

22
00:01:36.669 --> 00:01:42.419
RAII and smart pointers reduce risk, but they do not remove the need for design clarity.

23
00:01:42.419 --> 00:01:49.966
Secure C++ code should make ownership visible in types, keep borrowing short and obvious, and treat dangling references, stale pointers, unclear

24
00:01:49.966 --> 00:01:53.200
shared ownership, and cleanup during failure as review priorities.
